2 CONT Musa ol Ghasem - septieme Emam de la religion chiite / Conteste par les Ismailiens
2 CONT
2 CONT A grandi en prison pour le rendre plus faible. Il n'est pas reconnu par les ismaéliens.
2 CONT
2 CONT À l’âge de 20, en 148 A.H. après l'empoisonnement de son père, il devient imam. Il le reste pendant 35 ans, jusqu'à 183 A.H. 
2 CONT
2 CONT L’un des sujets importants de l’âge de l’imamat de Musa al-kazim est l’apparition de schismes comme l'ismaélisme, les Mubarakia, les Navoussia, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le pouvoir abbasside voulait savoir qui serait le successeur de Ja`far as-Sadiq. Cet héritier désigné était Ismaïl, ce qui l'obligeait à se protéger, tandis que son frère Mûsâ pouvait vivre de manière plus publique à Médine. La di
2 CONC sparition d'Ismaël (qui se serait occulté selon les ismaéliens) avant son père rendit cette succession encore plus confuse. À la mort de Ja`far as-Sadiq, pour la majorité, Mûsâ est devenu l'héritier, mais certains pensaient que la
2 CONC  succession revenait aux enfants d'Ismaël, ce qui créa les ismaéliens. Cette branche ne reconnaît pas l'imam al-Kazim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le vendredi 7 Safar de l’an 183 A.H., Musa al-Kazim, à l’âge de 55 ans, est empoisonné dans sa prison à Bagdad, sur ordre de Hâroun ar-Rachîd. Il est enterré en Irak dans un quartier de Bagdad, al-Kadhimiya.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Mūsá ibn Ja‘far al-Kāzim (Arabic: موسى بن جعفر الكاظم‎‎), also called Abūl-Hasan, Abū Abd Allah, Abū Ibrāhīm, and al-Kāzim (the one who controls his anger), was the seventh Shiite Imam after his father Ja'far al-Sadiq. He is regar
2 CONC ded by Sunnis as a renowned scholar, and was a contemporary of the Abbasid caliphs Al-Mansur, Al-Hadi, Al-Mahdi and Harun al-Rashid. He was imprisoned several times, finally dying in Baghdad in the Sindi ibn Shahak prison. Ali al-
2 CONC Ridha, the eighth Imām, and Fatemah Masume were among his children
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musa_al-Kadhim
2 CONT
2 CONT Mūsá ibn Ja‘far al-Kāzim (Arabic: موسى بن جعفر الكاظم‎), also called Abūl-Hasan, Abū Abd Allah, Abū Ibrāhīm, and al-Kāzim (the one who controls his anger), was the seventh Shiite Imam after his father Ja'far al-Sadiq. He is regard
2 CONC ed by Sunnis as a renowned scholar, and was a contemporary of the Abbasid caliphs Al-Mansur, Al-Hadi, Al-Mahdi and Harun al-Rashid. He was imprisoned several times; finally dying in Baghdad in the Sindi ibn Shahak prison. Ali al-R
2 CONC idha, the eighth Imām, and Fatemah Masume were among his children
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT [The anti-Shiʿite policy was pursued in Iraq itself, where, in 179/795-96, the politically neutral and quietist Seventh Imam, Musā Kāẓem, was arrested and imprisoned. Despite being shielded from the caliph’s wrath to some extent b
2 CONC y Yaḥyā Barmaki and his son Fażl, Musā died in captivity in 183/799 or shortly thereafter, either due to natural causes, or, according to Shiʿite sources, by poisoning.] ref http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/harun-al-rasid
2 CONT
