1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Seyyedeh Fatima is also listed as the wife of Toghril Beg and as the daughter of Al-Qa'im on a list of Seljuk sultans from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_sultans_of_the_Seljuq_Empire '''Wikipedia'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Seyyedeh Fatima may be mentioned on page 259 in [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&pg=PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Zubeida+Khatun,+wife+of+Malik-Shah&source=bl&ots=5h6pZ2rimG&sig=reGhqSBaBsqKwuYCVdSrdzoi_6I&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vWwyVdW8
2 CONC EMnksAT_mIDwBQ&ved=0CDEQ6AEwAw#v=onepage&q=Aka&f=false '''Continuity and Change in Medieval Persia'''] by Ann K.S. Lambton:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The sultan's haram was often large and included his wives and concubines and their attendants and female slaves. His chief wives had their own diwans, persona iqtas and landed estates, and were sometimes able to transmit these by
2 CONC  inheritance. Some disposed of considerable funds as witnessed by their charitable benefactions. Toghril Beg's chief wife Altun-Jan held estates in Iraq. She died in 452/1060 and bequeathed her estates and pensions to the caliph's
2 CONC  daughter. Toghril assigned these to the latter when the marriage contract between him and the caliph's daughter was finally concluded in 454/1062..."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''NOTE:''' As Seyyedeh Fatima was the caliph's daughter and she went on to marry Toghril Beg according to the Wikipedia page cited above, it is likely Seyyedeh Fatima is the woman who inherited the lands of Altun Jan Khatun.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * The caliph's daughter is mentioned on page 142 of [https://books.google.com/books?id=BAN6ONlDkgIC&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q&f=false '''The Annals of the Saljuq Turks: Selections from Al-Kamil 
2 CONC Fi'l-Ta'rikh of Ibn Al-Athir'''], translated by D.S. Richards:
2 CONT
2 CONT The bride was then transferred to the Royal Palace in the middle of Safar [February 1063]. She sat on a throne that was clothed in gold. The sultan came into her presence and kissed the ground and did obeisance. She did not lift t
2 CONC he veil from her face nor did she rise. He placed a large amount of jewels and such like before her. Every day he continued so to come, wait upon her and then leave. He bestowed robes upon Amid al-Mulk, feasted for several days an
2 CONC d gave robes of honour to all the emirs. He showed signs of great joy.
