1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * According to Claude Cahen, author of [https://books.google.com/books?id=-FvJAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA39&lpg=PA39&dq=qaysar+shah&source=bl&ots=c8Fc3lmBo8&sig=d3slpiIzS9jr7spa061vo41BGvU&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiPzZDrworOAhVDGz
2 CONC 4KHae5D7AQ6AEIPzAG#v=onepage&q=qaysar%20shah&f=false '''The Formation of Turkey: The Seljukid Sultanate of Rum, Eleventh to Fourteenth Century,'''] the mother of Kay Khusraw I was a Greek woman, though he does not give her name.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultanate_of_Rum Sultanate of Rûm]'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaykhusraw_I '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Kaykhusraw I''' (Old Anatolian Turkish: كَیخُسرو, Persian: غياث الدين كيخسرو بن قلج ارسلان‎, Ghiyāth ad-Dīn Kaykhusraw bin Qilij Arslān), the eleventh and youngest son of Kilij Arslan II, was Seljuk Sultan of Rûm. He succeeded 
2 CONC his father in 1192, but had to fight his brothers for control of the Sultanate. He ruled it 1192-1196 and 1205-1211.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married a daughter of Manuel Maurozomes, son of Theodore Maurozomes and of an illegitimate daughter of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos. Manuel Maurozomes fought on behalf of Kaykhusraw in 1205 and 1206.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1207 he seized Antalya from its Frankish garrison and furnished the Seljuq state with a port on the Mediterranean.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Niketas Choniates, he was killed in single combat by Theodore I Laskaris, the emperor of Nicaea, during the Battle of Antioch on the Meander.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son by Manuel Maurozomes' daughter, Kayqubad I, ruled the Sultanate from 1220 to 1237, and his grandson, Kaykhusraw II, ruled from 1237 to 1246.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Footnotes=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Charles M. Brand, "The Turkish Element in Byzantium, Eleventh-Twelfth Centuries", Dumbarton Oaks Papers, 43 (1989), p. 18.
2 CONT * Niketas Choniates, Orationes 172.1-10.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Bibliography=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Varzos, K. (1984), Ē genealogia tōn Komnēnōn, Thessaloniki, pp. 496–502.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Same profile as https://www.geni.com/people/Kaykhusraw-I-Sultan-of-R%C3%BBm/6000000031544214022?through=6000000041170560272
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdu'l Harij Muhammad Mughis ad-din Tughril Shah
2 CONT
2 CONT [The consort of Queen Rusudan was a younger son of 'Abdu'l Harij Muhammad Mughis ad-din Tughril Shah, the Seljuq emir of Erzurum, and his wife, a daughter of Sayf al-Din Begtimur, the ruler of Ahlat.[1] Tughril Shah had received E
2 CONC lbistan in appanage upon the division of the sultanate of Rum by his father Kilij Arslan II in 1192, but he exchanged it, c. 1201, for Erzurum.[2] He appears to have been a tributary to Georgia for at least parts of his reign.
2 CONT (ref:'Abdu'l Harij Muhammad Mughis ad-din Tughril Shah, the Seljuq emir of Erzurum) https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghias_ad-din#Ancestry]
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaykhusraw I (Old Anatolian Turkish: كَیخُسرو or Ghiyāth ad-Dīn Kaykhusraw bin Qilij Arslān; Persian: غياث الدين كيخسرو بن قلج ارسلان‎‎), the eleventh and youngest son of Kilij Arslan II, was Seljuk Sultan of Rûm. He succeeded his
2 CONC  father in 1192, but had to fight his brothers for control of the Sultanate. He ruled it 1192-1196 and 1205-1211.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married a daughter of Manuel Maurozomes,[1] son of Theodore Maurozomes and of an illegitimate daughter of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos. Manuel Maurozomes fought on behalf of Kaykhusraw in 1205 and 1206.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1207 he seized Antalya from its Frankish garrison and furnished the Seljuq state with a port on the Mediterranean.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Niketas Choniates, he was killed in single combat by Theodore I Laskaris, the emperor of Nicaea, during the Battle of Antioch on the Meander.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son by Manuel Maurozomes' daughter, Kayqubad I, ruled the Sultanate from 1220 to 1237, and his grandson, Kaykhusraw II, ruled from 1237 to 1246.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Kaykhusraw I (Old Anatolian Turkish: كَیخُسرو or Ghiyāth ad-Dīn Kaykhusraw bin Qilij Arslān; Persian: غياث الدين كيخسرو بن قلج ارسلان‎‎), the eleventh and youngest son of Kilij Arslan II, was Seljuk Sultan of Rûm. He succeeded his
2 CONC  father in 1192, but had to fight his brothers for control of the Sultanate. He ruled it 1192-1196 and 1205-1211.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married a daughter of Manuel Maurozomes, son of Theodore Maurozomes and of an illegitimate daughter of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos. Manuel Maurozomes fought on behalf of Kaykhusraw in 1205 and 1206.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1207 he seized Antalya from its Frankish garrison and furnished the Seljuq state with a port on the Mediterranean.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Niketas Choniates, he was killed in single combat by Theodore I Laskaris, the emperor of Nicaea, during the Battle of Antioch on the Meander.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son by Manuel Maurozomes' daughter, Kayqubad I, ruled the Sultanate from 1220 to 1237, and his grandson, Kaykhusraw II, ruled from 1237 to 1246.
