2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ancestry From Father'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdul Qadir al-Jilani son of Abu Salih Musa son of Abi Abdullah son of Yahya Zahid son of Muhammad son of Dawud son of Musa Sani son of Abdullah Sani son of Musa al-Jaun son of Abdullah al-Mahaz son of Hasan Musanna son of Hasan i
2 CONC bn Ali
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ancestry From Mother'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdul Qadir al-Jilani son of Um-ul-Khayr Fatima daughter of Abdullah as-Soma'i Muhammad son of Abu Mahmood Muhammad Tahir son of Abdul Ata Abdullah son of Abu Kamaluddin Isa son of Muhammad al-Taqi son of Ali al-Ridha son of Musa 
2 CONC al-Kadhim son of Jaffar al-Sadiq son of Muhammad al-Baqir son of Zayn al-Abidin son of Husayn ibn Ali
2 CONT
2 CONT Within al-Jilani's full name, al-Sayyid Muhiyudin Abu Muhammad Abdal Qadir al-Jilani al-Hasani wal-Hussaini, the word Sayyid denotes his descent from Muhammad.[10] The name Muhiyudin describes him as a "reviver of religion".[11] T
2 CONC he phrase, al-Jilani refers to Al Gilani's region of birth.[12][13] However, Al-Gilani also carried the epithet, al-Baghdadi.[14][15][16] referring to his residence and burial in Baghdad. The phrase al-Hasani wal-Hussaini affirms 
2 CONC his lineal descent from both Hasan ibn Ali and Hussein ibn Ali, the grandsons of Muhammad.[3][17]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Paternal Heritage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdul Qadir al-Jilani's father was a sharif.[18][19] He was respected as a saint would be, by the people of his day and was known as "Jangi Dost", (one who loves God), thus "Jangidost" was his sobriquet.[20][1] [21]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Maternal Heritage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdul Qadir al-Jilani's mother, Umm al-Khair Fatima, was the daughter of Sayyid Abdullah Sawmai az-Zaid and Zain ul Abideen[22][23][24][25] Abideen was known as,  "a great saint of his time and a direct descendant of Hazrat Imam H
2 CONC usain, the great martyr of Karbala."[26] Abideen professed his greatness as a Sufi scholar,
2 CONT
2 CONT "I am Hassani and my abode is my cell, And my feet are on the neck of each Saint. Abdul Qdir is my famous name. And my ancestor is one possessed of an insight Perfect." (Qaseedada-tul-Gausia).  Education
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdul Qadir al-Jilani spent his early life in Naif, the town of his birth. In 1095, at the age of eighteen years, Al Gilani went to Baghdad. There, he pursued the study of Hanbali law. Abu Ali al-Mukharrimi gave Al Gilani lessons 
2 CONC about Fiqh. He was given lessons about Hadith by Abu Bakr ibn Muzaffar. He was given lessons about Tafsir by Abu Muhammad Ja'far, a commentator. In Tasawwuf, his spiritual instructor was Abu'l-Khair Hammad ibn Muslim al-Dabbas. Af
2 CONC ter completing his education, Gilani left Baghdad. He spent twenty-five years as a reclusive wanderer in the desert regions of Iraq.[27]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Later Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1127, al-Jilani returned to Baghdad and began to preach in public. He joined the teaching staff of the school belonging to his teacher al-Mukharrimii and was popular with students. In the morning he taught hadith and tafsir, an
2 CONC d in the afternoon held discourse on the science of the hearts and the virtues of the Qur'an. From 521 to 561 AH.
2 CONT
2 CONT During this period hundreds of thousands of people converted to Islam because of him and organized several teams to go abroad for dawah purposes.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT al-Jilani was also the teacher of Ibn Qudamah. Al Gilani made Qudamah a Caliph of his Sufi order. Qudamah later fought as a general in Sultan Saladin Ayyubi's army which took rule in Jerusalem from the Christians.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death & Burial'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Al Galini died in the evening on Saturday 1166 (11 Rabi'us sani 561AH) at the age of ninety-one years (by the Islamic calendar).[28] His body was entombed in a shrine within his madrassa in Babul-Sheikh, Resafa (East bank of the T
2 CONC igris) in Baghdad, Iraq.[29][30][31] Worldwide, the Sufi orders celebrate "Ghouse-al-azham day" on al-Jilani's birthday rather than on his date of death.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Legacy'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdul Qadir al-Jilani continued the spiritual chain of ''Junayd Baghdadi''. His contribution to thought in the Muslim world earned him the title Muhiyuddin, meaning, "the reviver of the faith". al-Jilani, along with his students a
2 CONC nd associates laid the groundwork for the society which later produced stalwarts like Nur ad-Din and Saladin.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Descendants'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdul Qadir al-Jilani took four wives. He had forty-nine children, twenty-seven sons and twenty-two daughters.[citation needed] Among his sons are: Shaikh Abdul-Wahab, Sheikh Abdul-Razzaq, Shaikh Abdul-Aziz, Shaikh Isa, Shaikh Mus
2 CONC a, Sheikh Yahya, Sheikh Abdullah, Sheikh Muhammed and Sheikh Ibrahim. Since al-Jilani's time, his sons and grandsons have travelled to the Indian sub-continent to preach Islam by his method (Arabic=Tareqa,طريقة).One of the most fa
2 CONC mous descendents is Syed Abdul Razzaq Nur-ul-Alyn who is the nephew and Sajjada Nasheen of Ashraf Jahangir Semnani starting the Qadiriyyah Ashrafiya Silsila. They have reached North Africa, Morocco, and parts of the Horn of Africa
2 CONC . These areas include Ethiopia, Eritrea, and Somalia. In Somalia, the Qadiriyyah order is dominant although there are small areas where Ahmed ibn Idris' order is found. Again, in Somalia, the order is divided into the Zaylaiyyah o
2 CONC rder and the Uwaisiyyah order. The daughters of Syed Jalaluddin Hasan (Raees Hasan and Anees Khan) are the custodians of al-Jilani's namaz (prayer) cap (the "Kulhe Mubarak"). A ziyarat (pilgrimage) is made for al-Jilani each year.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In Defence of The Imaam,'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir Jeelaanee (d.561H) 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Rahimahullah)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://ahlulhadeeth.wordpress.com/category/in-defense-of-ahlul-hadeeth/
2 CONT
2 CONT Compiled
2 CONT
2 CONT Series compilers and Trans: Abu Hibbaan & Abu Khuzaimah Ansaari
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' Introduction'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahlul-Bid’ah Waz-Zaigh have attributed all sorts of heretical and innovative beliefs to Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir Jeelaanee, more so in our times especially by the soofee’s of the world, the bareilwee’s the deobandee’s. Some of these in
2 CONC dividuals have distorted and misinterpreted the teachings of the Shaikh in as much portraying the shaikh to be from amongst the propagator’s of Soofism, Wahdatul-Wajood, Fana, Baqa and Wahdatash-Shahood and other heretical beliefs
2 CONC . These individuals from Zaytuna institute in California, the translator of the reliance of the traveler and their foot soldiers are all upon this, this is their way and this what they propagate. So we humbly ask them show us the 
2 CONC uniformity of your beliefs and the beliefs of Shaikh Abdul Qaadir Jeelaanee. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''His Life (Summarised)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir Jeelaanee’s full name is Abdul-Qaadir bin Abee Saaleh Abdullah bin Jankee Daust al-Jailee (al-Jeelaanee). His kunyah was Abu Muhammad and his title’s were Muhi ud deen and Shaikh ul-Islaam. (Siyar A’laam an-Nab
2 CONC ula (20/439), al-Bidaayah Wan-Nihaayah (12/252). Fawaat al-Wafyaat (2/373), Shadhraat adh-Dhahab (4/198).
2 CONT
2 CONT Imaam Sama’anee referred to him as Imaam al-Hanaabillah (Imaam of the Hanbalee’s) (Dhail A’la Tabqaat al-Hanaabillah (1/291) of Ibn Rajab)
2 CONT
2 CONT The author of Shadhraat traced his lineage to Hussain bin Alee (Radhiallaahu Anhuma) and he was born in 471H and according to some 470H in the place of Jeelaan. (Siyar A’laam an-Nabula (20/439).
2 CONT
2 CONT Imaam Dhahabee mentioned he came to Baghdaad in his childhood. (Siyar A’laam an-Nabula (20/439).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''His Teachers and Students'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Haafidh Dhahabee mentioned the following as his teachers. Qaadheee Abu Sa’eed Makhramee, Abu Ghaalib (Muhammad bin Hussain) Baqalaanee, Ahmad bin Muzaffar bin Saus, Abu Qaasim bin Bayaan, Ja’afar bin Ahmad Siraaj, Abu Sa’ad bin Kh
2 CONC asheesh, Abu Taalib Yoosufee and others. (Siyar A’laam an-Nabula (20/440).
2 CONT
2 CONT From his students Imaam Dhahabee has mentioned, Abu Sa’ad Sama’anee, Umar bin Alee Qurashee, Shaikh Muwaffiq ud deen ibn Qudaamah (the author of the monumental book, Lu’matul Ei’tiqaad),  Abdur-Razzaaq bin Abdul-Qaadir and Moosaa 
2 CONC bin Abdul Qaadir (both are the sons of the Shaikh), Alee bin Idrees, Ahmad bin Mu’tee Abu Hurairah, Muhammad bin Laith Wastaanee, Akmal bin Mas’ood Haashimee, Abu Taalib Abdul-Lateef bin Muhammad bin Qabeetee and others. (Siyar A’
2 CONC laam an-Nabula (20/440).
2 CONT
2 CONT After learning the Shaikh began to admonish and cultivate the people with sincerity. As a result Allaah aided him and put much blessing in his work and his circles and his admonishes became very famous, to the extent that the rule
2 CONC rs of the time would also participate in his circles aswell as major people of knowledge of his time. The affect of the Shaikh’s admonishing was such that many a people repented for their sins and mistakes. (Siyar A’laam an-Nabula
2 CONC  (20/441).
2 CONT
2 CONT Haafidh Ibn Katheer said, “After he came to Baghdaad he started seeking knowledge of hadeeth and Fiqh from Abu Sa’eed Makhramee Hanbalee. Abu Sa’eed had a small institution, which he handed over to Shaikh Abdul Qaadir. In this ins
2 CONC titution the Shaikh would deliver lectures of knowledge and would admonish the people, many people benefited from him.” (al-Bidaayah Wan-Nihaayah (12/252).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Shaikh died in 561H and the age of 90. (Siyar A’laam an-Nabula (20/450)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Shaikh authored a number of books (which will be quoted from) however many books are also attributed to him which he himself did not write and it is these books which Ahlul-Bid’ah have utilized in disparaging the Shaikh and hi
2 CONC s Aqeedah.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Al-Ghuniyyah ut-Taalibeen'''
2 CONT
2 CONT This the Shaikh’s most famous work and his beliefs, his way and ideology are mentioned in this book. Its proper name chosen by the Shaikh as al-Ghuniyyah ut-Taaliben    Tareeq al-Haqq. However some latter people (from the Soofiyya
2 CONC h) have denounced this book and denied it to be a work of the Shaikh on the account of its contents. So in order to repel this fallacy, Haajee Khaleefah said,
2 CONT
2 CONT “al-Ghuniyyatuh ut-Taalibeen Tareeq al-Haqq is the work of Abul-Qaadir Jeelaanee who died in 561H.” (Kashf adh-Dhanoon (2/121).
2 CONT
2 CONT Haafidh Ibn Katheer and Shaikh ul-Islaam Imaam Ibn Taymiyyah have mentioned this to be the book of Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir Jeelaanee (al-Bidaayah Wan-Nihaayah (12/252), Majmoo Fatwaa (5/15).
2 CONT
2 CONT He also wrote Fatooh al-Gayb, Fath ur-Rabbaanee Wal-Faidh ar-Rehmaanee and a number of other works.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Shaikhs Belief’s and Teachings'''
2 CONT
2 CONT From the works of the Shaikh his belief’s and teaching were the same as Ahlus-Sunnah Wal-Jama’ah. The Shaikh said,
2 CONT
2 CONT “My belief’s are the belief’s which were upheld by the Companions and the (Salaf us-Saaleh) Pious Predecessors.” (Siyar A’laam an-Nabula (20/442).
2 CONT
2 CONT Similarly he would also instruct and advise others to adhere to this madhab and the belief of the companions and Salaf us-Saaleh, he would say,
2 CONT
2 CONT “Upon you is Ittibaa (following and obeying) and not innovation and upon you is the madhab of the Salaf as-Saaleh and this is the straight path you should firmly adhere to.” (al-Fath ur-Rabbaanee (p.35)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He also said'''
2 CONT
2 CONT “It is upon the believer to follow and obey the Sunnah and those upon the Sunnah, the Jama’ah. The Sunnah is what the Messenger of Allaah (Sallalahu Alayhee Was-Sallam) declared to be Sunnah and the Jama’ah is that, which the comp
2 CONC anions are agreed upon.” (al-Ghuniyyah (1/165).. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Concerning Eemaan'''
2 CONT
2 CONT There are differences in the Issue of Eemaan between Ahlus-Sunnah and the deviated sects. Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir explained Eemaan just as Ahlus-Sunnah described it. He said,
2 CONT
2 CONT “We believe Eemaan is the statement of the tongue, the affirmation of the heart and action of the limbs, it increases with obedience and decreases with disobedience. It is strengthened with knowledge and weakened with ignorance…” 
2 CONC (al-Ghuniyyah (1/135).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Concerning Tawheed'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Shaikh said concerning Tawheed ar-Raboobiyyah and Tawheed al-Uloohiyyah,
2 CONT
2 CONT “The soul is inclined to obey its creator because The Rabb is his creator and master and it (the soul) is dependent in worshipping its Rabb.” (Fatooh al-Gayb (pg.21)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He also said'''
2 CONT
2 CONT “The person who wishes to enter into Islaam, it is obligatory upon him that he must first say the Shahadah that None has the right to be worshipped except Allaah and Muhammad (Sallalahu Alayhee Was-Sallam) is his Messenger. He mus
2 CONC t also dissociate himself from all the other religions other than Islaam and believe in the oneness of Allaah with his heart.” (al-Ghuniyyah (1/13).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Concerning Asmaa Was-Sifaat'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Shaikh said'''
2 CONT
2 CONT “In regard to this we do not leave the Book and the Sunnah. We recite the verses and the narrations and have faith in them and we leave the kayfiyyah (howness-of these) attributes to Allaah.” (al-Ghuniyyah (1/125).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Shaikh has refuted the deviated sects on their beliefs of the attributes of Allaah in detail in his book (al-Ghuniyyah (1/125-140) so please refer to it.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Concerning The Qur’aan'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir said'''
2 CONT
2 CONT “Our belief is the Qur’aan is the speech of Allaah, the holy book and it is revelation which was revealed upon the Messenger of Allaah (Sallalahu Alayhee-Wasallam) by Jibraa’eel.” (al-Ghuniyyah (1/125-140).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In Refutation of Shirk and Innovations'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Shaikh was a firm propagator of Tawheed and a destroyer of Shirk and innovations as was evident from his statements. Some of them are,
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.      “Man should supplicate to Allaah, praise and glorify him, then send salutations upon the Messenger of Allaah and then finally ask Allaah for his needs.” (al-Ghuniyyah (1/92).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.      “It is unlawful to swear by anyone other than Allaah so if someone wishes to swear then let him do so by Allaah only or otherwise let him remain silent.” (al-Ghuniyyah (1/92).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.      “When you visit the graves then do not put your hands on them or kiss them as this is the habit of the jews, nor sit on the graves or rest against them. Then ask Allaah for your needs.” (al-Ghuniyyah (1/91).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.      “Follow and obey and do not innovate. Agree with the religion and do not differ, obey and do not disobey, have sincerity and do not do Shirk.  Accept Allaah as one and do not turn away from him. Ask Allaah and do not ask o
2 CONC thers. Ask him for help and do not anyone else for help. Have reliance and trust in him and do not have reliance in anyone else.” (al-Fath ur-Rabbaanee (pg.151).
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.      “The whole creation is dependent upon him and helpless without him. No can benefit or harm you…” (Fath ur-Rabbaanee (pg.89).
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh ul-Islaam Imaam Ibn Taymiyyah On Shaikh Abdul Qaadir.
2 CONT
2 CONT It also appears the Shaikh was an upholder of the Salafee Aqeedah as he has in his al-Ghuniyyah refuted and censured all the deviated sects from the Shee’ah Rawaafidh, Murjee’ah, Qadariyyah, Jahmiyyah, Karamiyyah, Mu’tazilah and o
2 CONC thers. The only one left are the Ashaabul Hadeeth or Ahlus-Sunnah and it is this group of people the Shaikh informed the people to adhere to.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh ul-Islaam in his refutation of the deviated sects, and he has a recognized status in this, used the statements of Shaikh Abul-Qaadir as supports and witnesses. (see Majmoo Fataawa (5/85), (10/455, 524, 548), (11/604).
2 CONT
2 CONT So if there were any problems with the Aqeedah of Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir then Shaikh ul-Islaam Ibn Taymiyyah would have most definitely indicated or clarified them. Rather, we see the contrary to this as he refers and addresses Shaik
2 CONC h Abdul-Qaadir al-Jeelaanee as, The Major Shaikh, ash-Shaikh, the Imaam, One of the Imaams and so on. (see Majmoo Fataawa (5/85), (11/604).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Zuhd and Tasawwuf'''
2 CONT
2 CONT It should be known that concepts such as Hulool, Wahdatal-Wajood, Wahdash-Shahood and other were found amongst the soofee’s like Ibn Arabee and Abdul-Kareem Jailee, the later soofees and in Mansoor al-Hallaaj. This is the reason w
2 CONC hy earlier books of so-called Soofee’s had correct Aqeedah in them also but they also fell short in aspects of purifying their souls.
2 CONT
2 CONT The term soofism was not found amongst the earlier generations especially during the first period. However in the second and third periods the words Zuhd, Aabid and Saaleh would be used for highly pious people and later the word s
2 CONC oofee began being used for these people. (See Majmoo Fataawa (6/11).
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir has a detailed discussion in his al-Ghuniyyah (2/269-336) which is nothing but concerning Zubd and Taqwaa, trust, hope, love and truthfulness in Allaah.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Shaikh himself refuted Wahdatal-Wajood and said,
2 CONT
2 CONT “Allaah rises over his throne.. Allaah is upon his throne…..and he is separate from the creation and nothing is hidden from his knowledge and it is not correct to say he is present everywhere and this is what Allaah has mentioned 
2 CONC in the Qur’aan, “The most Merciful rose over the Throne.” (Tahaa:5)” (al-Ghuniyyah (1/121-124).
2 CONT
2 CONT Karamah And Miracles (Mu’ajizah) 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Difference between the two is that a miracle is performed at the hands of the Prophets like the splitting of the moon by our Messenger (Sallahu Alayhee Was-Sallam) and the cooling of the Fire for Ibraaheem (Alyahis-Salaam). A 
2 CONC karamah on the other hand is something amazing performed at the hands of a Walee. Howeverthe following need to be understood,
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.      A mu’ajizah is performed at the hand of a prophet and a karamah in the hands of a Walee.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.      Just as a walee’s status cannot reach the status of a Prophet, similarly a walee’s karamah cannot be considered to be synonymous with a Prophets miracle. (Dalaa’il an-Nabuwwah (pgs.109-112).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.      The Prophets or walee’s do not have any authority in performing the miracles or karamah’s, their occurrence is due to the command of Allaah. (Soorah al-Israa:90-93).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.      It is not permissible for a muslim at all to deny a miracle of a Prophet however a walee’s karamah can be accepted OR rejected. (See Majmoo Fataawa (11/208).
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no doubt Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir was a pious and righteous individual and it is possible some of the karamah’s attributed to him maybe correct, however so many have been attributed to him by extreme soofee’s who pass the limi
2 CONC ts and enter the realms of Ghuloo in doing so and most of these are without evidence or authentic chains. So the affair is that many of the karamah’s attributed to him are not true but mere blind love of the soofee’s who forged th
2 CONC em and attributed them to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Haafidh Dhahabee said,
2 CONT
2 CONT “I say there is not a shaikh from the major Shayookh’s whose karamah’s are more well known than the karamah’s of Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir, however most of them are not correct and some of them are from the things which are impossible.”
2 CONC  (Siyar (20/450).
2 CONT
2 CONT Haafidh Ibn Katheer also said something very similar. (al-Bidaayah Wan-Nihaayah (12/252).
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of the people who had extreme love for Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir compiled his karamah’s in a book. One such person was Alee bin Yoosuf ash-Shatnoofee who died 150 years after the death of Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir in approximately 713H. 
2 CONC (see al-A’laam (5/188), Kashf adh-dhanoon (1/257).
2 CONT
2 CONT Shatnoofee mentioned the karamah’s of Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir in such a manner as if he was from the Shaikh’s time and the reader is fooled into thinking this. Shatnoofee also attempted to mention these karamah’s with his own chains a
2 CONC nd most of these are weak due to the narrators in the chain. This is the reason why the research scholars reprimanded Shatnoofee strongly.
2 CONT
2 CONT So Haafidh Ibn Hajr mentioned from Shaikh al-Kamaal Ja’afar, “Shatnoofee has mentioned very strange and odd things in this book and the people have criticized most of the incidences he has mentioned and their chains.” (ad-Durr al-
2 CONC Kaaminah (3/142).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn al-Wardee mentioned in his Taareekh, “Bahjatul-Israar (the name of Shatnoofee’s book) contains such things which cannot be accepted and such exaggerated things have been mentioned about Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir that are not possibl
2 CONC e for anyone except Allaah.” (Kashf adh-Dhanoon (1/257).
2 CONT
2 CONT Haafidh Ibn Rajab said, “Shatnoofee has written a three volume book on Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir and in it he has compiled a mountain of lies. Whereas it is sufficient for a person be declared a liar for him to narrate everything he hea
2 CONC rs. I have seen some of the quotes in the book but my soul was not content in believing them because firstly the narrations have been taken from unknown people. Secondly not only are their mountains of lies and allegations on Shai
2 CONC kh Abdul-Qaadir but it is also contrary to the status of the Shaikh if attributed to him. The statement of Shaikh al-Kamaal has also passed by me where he says the things Shanoofee has mentioned in his book Bahjatul-Israar have ca
2 CONC used him to be accused (of lying).” (Dhail Tabaqaat (1/293) of Ibn Rajab). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir Jeelaanee also had mistakes like every son of Adam, Haafidh Dhahabee the student of Imaam Ibn Taymiyyah described them as,
2 CONT
2 CONT “The summary is Shaikh Abdul-Qaadir was a man of great respect however some of his statements still need looking into and weighed up, which we leave to Allaah, and from some of them are lies which have been attributed to him.” (Si
2 CONC yar (20/451).
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh ul-Islaam Imaam Ibn Taymiyyah said, “Such statements and actions have been mentioned from some of the Mujtahid’s from the Salaf us-Saaliheen which are included as innovations. However the people of knowledge did not take th
2 CONC em as innovations rather they took them on the basis of weak narrations thinking the narration was authentic. Similarly they extrapolated from verses and these extrapolations were not correct and they were unable to find specific 
2 CONC texts in some of these issues. When someone has fear of Allaah in his heart then he falls under the saying of Allaah, “Oh Rabb if w err or make a mistake then do not account us for it.” And the Saheeh mentions, “Allaah says I acce
2 CONC pt.” (Majmoo Fataawa (19/191).
2 CONT
2 CONT http://ahlulhadeeth.wordpress.com/category/in-defense-of-ahlul-hadeeth/
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hadhrat Shaikh Sayyid 'Abd al-Qadir al-Hasani al-Hussaini al-Jilani was born in ramazan 471H (CE:1078) in a small town of Gilan Province, to Syed Abu-Saleh Musa al-Hasani and Syeda Fatima. Shaikh's father passed away a few days af
2 CONC ter his birth. Shaikh was a descendant of Muhammad(SAW) from both of his parents - his father was a descendant of Imam Hasan and his mother was a descendant of Imam Husayn, the most beloved grand sons of our beloved prophet(SAW). 
2 CONC Hence he is called, al-Hasani wal Husaini. The family tree of the Shaikh is given below:
2 CONT
2 CONT Syed Abdul-Qadir, son of Syed Abu-Saleh Musa, son of Syed Zahid, son of Syed Abdullah al-jeeli, son of Syed Yahya Az-Zahid Syed Muhammad Syed Dawood Syed Musa Al-joon Syed Abdullah Al-Mahadh(Al-Majal) Syed Hasan Al-Mathnaa Syed Ha
2 CONC san Ibn Ali Ali Ibn Abi_Talib (Fourth rightly guided Caliph)
2 CONT
2 CONT His spiritual chain is Shaikh Abdul Qadir Jilani, disciple of Shaikh Qadi Abi Said Al-Mubarak al-Mukhrami, disciple of Shaikh Abul Hasan Ali Ahmad Qareshi al-Hankari, disciple of Shaikh Abu Farah Muhammad Yusuf Tartusi, disciple o
2 CONC f Shaikh Raziuddin Abul Fazl Abdul Wahid Abdul Aziz, disciple of Shaikh Abu Bakr Abdullah Shibli, disciple of Shaikh Abul Qasim Junaid of Bagdad, disciple of Shaikh Abul Hasan Siri Saqti, disciple of Shaikh Maroof Al-Karkhi, disci
2 CONC ple of Shaikh Sulaiman Dawood Tai, disciple of Shaikh Habib ul Ajami, disciple of Shaikh Hasan al-Basri, disciple of Sayyidina Ali ibn Abi Talib, Khalifa of Sayyidina Muhammad Ibn Abdullah (Peace Be Upon Him), The Prophet of Allah
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on Saturday night 1166 (8th Rabi' al-Awwal 561AH on the Islamic calendar) at the age of eighty-nine years, and was entombed in a shrine within his Madrassa in Baghdad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of his works are given below:
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Ghunya li-talibi tariq al-haqq wa al-din (Sufficient Provision for Seekers of the Path of Truth and Religion) Al-Fath ar-Rabbani (The Sublime Revelation) Malfuzat (Utterances) Futuh al-Ghaib (Revelations of the Unseen) Jala' al
2 CONC -Khatir (The Removal of Care)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://anwariya.com/Pages/Our%20Tradition.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Abdul-Qadir Gilani From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Question book-new.svg 	This article needs additional citations for verification. Please help improve this article by adding reliable references. Unsourced material may be ch
2 CONC allenged and removed. (March 2011) 	This article may contain original research. Please improve it by verifying the claims made and adding references. Statements consisting only of original research may be removed. More details may
2 CONC  be available on the talk page. (March 2011)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jilani redirects here. For others of the same name, see Gilani Abdul Qadir Gilani Religion 	Islam School 	Hanbali Personal Born 	1077 CE (Common Era) Gilan, Iran Died 	1166 CE Baghdad, Iraq Senior posting Title 	Sheikh, Ghaus-e-Az
2 CONC am Religious career Works 	Al-Ghunya li-talibi tariq al-haqq wa al-din, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Syed Muhiyudin Abu Muhammad Abdal Qadir al-Gilani al-Hasani wal-Hussaini ,[1][2] (Persian: عبد القادر گیلانی,Urdu: عبد القادر گیلانی Abdolqāder Gilāni) (also spelled Abdulqadir Gaylani, Abdelkader, Abdul Qadir, Abdul Khadir - J
2 CONC ilani, Jeelani, Jilali, Gailani, Gillani, Gilani, Al Gilani) or simply known as Ghaus-e-Azam (470–561 AH) (1077–1166 CE) was a Persian[3] Hanbali preacher, Sufi sheikh and the figurehead of the Qadiri Sufi order. He was born on a 
2 CONC Wednesday the 10th Rabi at-Thani in 470 AH, 1077 CE,[4] in the Persian province of Gilan (Iran) south of the Caspian Sea. Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT     1 Lineage and Biography
2 CONT         1.1 Education
2 CONT         1.2 Later life
2 CONT         1.3 Death
2 CONT     2 Family
2 CONT     3 Works
2 CONT     4 Bibliography
2 CONT     5 See also
2 CONT     6 List of notable personalities
