3 CITY Tarim
3 STAE Hadramaut
3 CTRY Yemen
1 OCCU Islamic scholar, Sufi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Muhammad al-Faqih al-Muqaddam'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī Bā ʿAlawī (Arabic: محمد بن علي باعلوي) commonly known as al-Faqīh al-Muqaddam. 574 H - 653 H or 1178 CE - 1232 CE) is known as the founder of Ba 'Alawiyya Sufi order[1] which has influenced Sufism in Yemen, South
2 CONC  Asia and Southeast Asia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muhammad_al-Faqih_al-Muqaddam
2 CONT
2 CONT He is the only son of Ali son of Muhammad Sahib al-Mirbath whom all 75 families of Ba 'Alawi sada that spread out from Yemen to Southeast Asia are rooted.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The Title al-Faqih was given because he was a great teacher and student of religion and jurisprudence.
2 CONT
2 CONT While the title al-Muqaddam means he is the foremost. In this case, Muhammad ibn Ali throughout his life was always given precedence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad was born in Tarim, Hadhramaut, Yemen.  His grave located in Zanbal [1] in Hadhramaut is frequently visited by Muslims often before they visit other religious sites in Yemen.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT His patrilineal lineage is
2 CONT
2 CONT # ''Muhammad''
2 CONT # Ali [Sayyid Ali ibn Muhammad Sahib al-Mirbat Ba' Alawi]
2 CONT # Muhammad Sahib al-Mirbat
2 CONT # Ali Khali Qasam
2 CONT # Alawi al-Thani
2 CONT # Muhammad Sahib al-Sawma'ah
2 CONT # Alawi al-Awwal
2 CONT # Ubayd Allah
2 CONT # Ahmad al-Muhajir
2 CONT # Isa al-Rumi
2 CONT # Muhammad al-Naqib
2 CONT # Ali al-Uraydi
2 CONT # Imam Ja'far al-Sadiq
2 CONT # Imam Muhammad al-Baqir
2 CONT # Imam Zayn al-Abidin
2 CONT # Imam Husayn
2 CONT # Imam Ali ibn Abi Talib
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:  Zaynab bint Ahmad ibn Muhammad Sahib al-Mirbat
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT # Alawi al-Ghayur
2 CONT # Ali
2 CONT # Ahmad
2 CONT # Abd Allah
2 CONT # Abd al-Rahman
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://id.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muhammad_al-Faqih_Muqaddam
2 CONT
2 CONT http://freepages.family.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~naqobatulasyrof/main/des/d18.htm#i8
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: AlHabib Muhammad was born in Tarim, Hadhramaut 574H. Muhammad died 653H in Tarim, Hadhramut.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad bin Ali (574H - 653H) was titled The Father of Tarim, The Great Professor and the Forefront Jurisprudence (Al Faqih Al Muqadam)., title and honour not given to any one before him. He was deeply versed in theology, exegesi
2 CONC s of the science of Traditions (Ilmu Al Hadith) of the Prophet, and in jurisprudence. He was an orator, a forceful instructor and an excellent interpreter of Islamic law (Mufti). He combined between traditional knowledge and ratio
2 CONC nal knowledge. AlHabib Muhammad Al Faqih had written a number of dissertations and a book, which consists of case study of 300 questions, which he was asked on various issues. He counts a number of eminent Imams as his students, a
2 CONC nd among them are Imam Abdullah bin Muhamad bin Abdul Rahman Baabad, Imam Abdul Rahman bin Muhamad bin Abdul Rahman Ba abad, Imam Abdalla bin Ibrahim Ba qshuayn, Imam Said bin Omar Ba'alhaaf, Imam Ibrahim bin Yahya Ba fadhil, Imam
2 CONC  Ali bin Muhamad Al Khatyb and his 5 sons, who all were noted Imams. As a Father of Tarim he was literally feeding the poor, giving them one thousand pound of dates daily. His house was a shelter to the refugees, strangers and tra
2 CONC velers. His wife Hababah Zainab was his right hand on his service to the people and giving out charity and annual alms (Zakat) until she was titled The Mother of the poor - Umi Al fuqaraa As a Spiritual Leader, AlHAbib Muhammad Al
2 CONC  Faqih Al Muqadam was the pioneer who introduced Sufism in Hadhramaut. His immense spiritual stature, scholarship, his compassionate attitude to the mass and a man of the people - The Father of Tarim, enable his new Sufi order to 
2 CONC have the mass followers of all his descendents and the people of Tarim and the entire province of Hadhramaut. The order was known as Ba-Alawi order. Sufism as a school of thought was not known in Tarim. AlHabib Muhammad Al Faqih A
2 CONC l Muqadam introduced and implemented Alawy order through his inward and outward spiritual connection. With regard to inward spiritual connections, authentic traditions have it that AlHabib Muhammad Al Faqih Al Muqadam was in const
2 CONC ant union with the Prophet and holy men who had passed away. As with regard to outward spiritual connections, AlHabib Muhammad Al Faqih Al Muqadam received two chains of spiritual connections up to the Prophet. The first chain of 
2 CONC connection is through Alawy connections, from his father, grand father and great grand father upwards up to the Prophet. The second connection was through orthodox Sufi chain, where he received a Khirqa from Abu Madyan Al Maghriby
2 CONC , and Abu Madyan received from Sheikh Aby Ya'azy Al Maghriby, and Aby Ya'azy from Sheikh Aby Al Hassan bin Harzham, and Aby Al Hassan from Imam Abu Bakar bin Abdullah bin Al Raby Al Maghafry, and Ibn Al Raby from Imam Al Ghazaly, 
2 CONC and Al Ghazaly from Imam Al Haramyn Abu Talib Al Maky, and Imam Al Haramyn from his father Imam Al Juwayni, and Imam Al Juwayni from Abu Talib Maky, and Abu Talib Maky from Shibly, and Shibly from Imam Al Junaedi, and Imam Junaedi
2 CONC  from Asyry Al Saqty, and Asyry Al Saqty from Maaruf Al Kharqy, and Maaruf Al Kharqy from Daud Al Tayi, and Daud Al Tayi from Habib Al Ajamy, and Habib Al Ajamy from AlHabib Hassan Al Busry, and AlHasan Hassan Al Busry from Imam A
2 CONC li ra., and Imam Ali ra. from Prophet Muhammad saw May Peace and Blessing of Allah be upon Him, and Prophet from Angel Jibril, and Jibril from The Almighy Allah. He had 5 sons; Alwi, Ahmad, Ali, Abdullah, and Abdurrahman.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Samal/Hadralmaut.
