1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firuz-Shah_Zarrin-Kolah
2 CONT
2 CONT Firuz Shah Zarrin Kolah (Persian: فیروزشاه زرین کلاه, literal meaning in English: King Firuz of golden crown‎) was a Kurdish dignitary,[1][2] and the seventh in the ancestral line of Shaykh Safi Ardabili, the eponym of the Safavid
2 CONC  dynasty of Iran.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the pre-Safavid written work Safvat as-safa (oldest known extants manuscripts from 1485 and 1491), the origin of the Safavids is traced to Firuz Shah Zarin Kolah who is called a Kurd (from sanjār), while in the post-Safavid man
2 CONC uscripts, this portion "Kurd from Sanjar" has been excised and Piruz Shah Zarin Kollah is made a descendant of the Shi'ite Imams[3] The male lineage of the Safavid family given by the oldest manuscript of the Safwat as-Safa is:"[S
2 CONC heykh] Safi al-Din Abul-Fatah Ishaaq the son of Al-Sheykh Amin al-din Jebrail the son of al-Saaleh Qutb al-Din Abu Bakr the son of Salaah al-Din Rashid the son of Muhammad al-Hafiz al-Kalaam Allah the son of ‘avaad the son of Biro
2 CONC oz (Firuz) al-Kurdi al-Sanjāri.
2 CONT
2 CONT Excerpt from the Safvat Al-Safa, which describes the lineage of Shaykh Safi al-Din and traces it to Firuz Shah Zarin Kolah
2 CONT
2 CONT Wa chon Nisbat Birooz bâ Kurd raft translates to "Since the origin of Birooz was Kurdish" Firuz Shah likely migrated from Kurdistan to the region of Ardabil in the 11th century.[1]
